                }
              ]
            }
          },
          "layers": [
            {
              "layerId": "slide_2_03_layer_1",
              "componentId": "slide_2_03_process",
              "index": 1,
              "audioFile": "m2_2_03_L1.mp3",
              "words": 65,
              "approxSeconds": 28,
              "lines": [
                "Stage one. Verify the physician's order, confirming the number of blood culture sets required, the collection sites, and the timing in relation to antibiotic administration. Identify the patient using at least two identifiers, such as name and hospital identification number or date of birth. Then explain the procedure - the purpose of the test, the possibility of brief discomfort, and the importance of remaining still."
              ]
            },
            {
              "layerId": "slide_2_03_layer_2",
              "componentId": "slide_2_03_process",
              "index": 2,
              "audioFile": "m2_2_03_L2.mp3",
              "words": 73,
              "approxSeconds": 31,
              "lines": [
                "Stage two. Perform hand hygiene by washing thoroughly or using an alcohol-based hand rub. Prepare the equipment: check the expiry date of the bottles, ensure the seals are intact, and verify that labels and the laboratory request form are ready. Disinfect the bottle tops by cleaning the rubber septa with 70 percent alcohol and allowing them to dry. Then select the venipuncture site, preferring a peripheral vein such as the median cubital vein."
              ]
            },
            {
              "layerId": "slide_2_03_layer_3",
              "componentId": "slide_2_03_process",
              "index": 3,
              "audioFile": "m2_2_03_L3.mp3",
              "words": 62,
              "approxSeconds": 27,
              "lines": [
                "Stage three. Apply the tourniquet and palpate the vein to identify a suitable site, releasing the tourniquet temporarily if needed. Disinfect the skin with chlorhexidine using a back-and-forth or circular motion and allow it to dry completely - and do not touch the site afterward. Put on sterile gloves to maintain aseptic technique. Then re-apply the tourniquet without contaminating the cleaned site."
              ]
            },
            {
              "layerId": "slide_2_03_layer_4",
              "componentId": "slide_2_03_process",
              "index": 4,
              "audioFile": "m2_2_03_L4.mp3",
              "words": 51,
              "approxSeconds": 22,
              "lines": [
                "Stage four. Perform the venipuncture and collect the recommended blood volume. Inoculate the blood culture bottles in the correct order for your device - aerobic first with a butterfly set, anaerobic first with a syringe. Then mix gently, inverting the bottles several times so the blood combines with the culture medium."
              ]
            },
            {
              "layerId": "slide_2_03_layer_5",
              "componentId": "slide_2_03_process",
              "index": 5,
              "audioFile": "m2_2_03_L5.mp3",
              "words": 59,
              "approxSeconds": 25,
              "lines": [
                "Stage five. Label at the bedside with the patient identifiers, date and time, collection site and your initials. Remove the needle, apply pressure with sterile gauze until bleeding stops, and cover the site with a clean dressing. Discard the needle immediately into a puncture-resistant sharps container, without recapping. Complete the documentation. And send the bottles to the laboratory immediately."
              ]
            }
          ],
